==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|la|en}} ''[in]fu(n)do''  ''fu(n)dere''  ''fusi''  ''fusum'': to pour.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|uːz}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|infus|ing}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To cause to become an [[element]] of something; to [[insert]] or [[fill]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[steep]] in a liquid, so as to [[extract]] the soluble [[constituent]]s (usually [[medicinal]] or [[herbal]]).
#* Coxe
#*: One scruple of dried leaves is '''infused''' in ten ounces of warm water.
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To inspire; to inspirit or animate; to fill (with).
#* Shakespeare
#*: '''Infuse''' his breast with magnanimity.
#* Shakespeare
#*: '''infusing''' him with self and vain conceit
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[instill]] as a quality.
#* Shakespeare
#*: That souls of animals '''infuse''' themselves / Into the trunks of men.
#* Jonathan Swift
#*: Why should he desire to have qualities '''infused''' into his son, which himself never possessed, or knew, or found the want of, in the acquisition of his wealth?
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To undergo [[infusion]].
#* ''Let it '''infuse''' for five minutes.''
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To make an infusion with (an ingredient); to [[tincture]]; to [[saturate]].
#: {{rfquotek|Francis Bacon}}
# {{cx|transitive|obsolete|lang=en}} To pour in, as a liquid; to pour (into or upon); to shed.
#* Denham
#*: That strong Circean liquor cease to '''infuse'''.

